## generating video script
2026-02-03 14:07:09 | DEBUG | "./app\services\task.py:86": generate_script - video script: 
Many people wonder what the most harmful type of alcohol is. In fact, 'how you drink' has a far more critical impact on your health than the specific type of alcohol itself. Whether it's soju, beer, or whiskey, reckless binge drinking habits are the most destructive to the liver and brain. Since our bodies process alcohol based on the amount of pure ethanol, not the type of drink, all alcohol becomes toxic when consumed in excess. Ultimately, the most harmful alcohol is one that leads you to consume a large amount of pure alcohol in a short period. Strong spirits like soju, vodka, and whiskey, especially when mixed with sweet drinks and consumed quickly or in rapid succession, flood the body with enormous amounts of alcohol in an instant. This rapid influx of alcohol severely damages the liver, causes extreme hangovers, and dramatically increases the risk of accidents. Our bodies take the biggest hit when concentrated ethanol is rapidly absorbed. You should be especially cautious of sweet cocktails or mixed drinks that mask the alcohol content. You unknowingly consume far more ethanol, and while you might feel less intoxicated at first, you ultimately face greater repercussions. Furthermore, cheap or unverified alcohol may contain more impurities like methanol in addition to ethanol, which can cause severe headaches and long-term health problems. These 'hidden' alcohols can lead to unexpected risks. Ultimately, the most harmful alcohol is any alcohol consumed indiscriminately, without limits, and without considering its effects on the body. Even light beer can become toxic if consumed excessively, while strong spirits can be acceptable if consumed in moderation. Always stick to an appropriate amount, drink plenty of water, and protect your precious health by avoiding drinking on an empty stomach. It's important to remember that no alcohol is safe when consumed irresponsibly.
